WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
Stinger, save the earth from an alien snack attack.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:24.440
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:25.460
I'm Mike.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.300
I'm Joe. And I'm Sean.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.880
Guys, we got a fun one today.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:32.280
Stinger on the NES, because that's all we ever do.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.600
I never really say the console.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:34.880
I usually just say the game.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:35.940
On the PS2.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.060
Right. I've been playing so much Kingdom Hearts 3 on PS4.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.540
You always have to say the game and then the console going forward.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.860
Right. You're really late to the game with that one, aren't you Mike?

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.740
Well, you know, it's no secret to a lot of people that we publish the episodes.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.240
That game came out three years ago.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.080
Honestly, probably, right?

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.300
What are we like?

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.520
Can we say how many episodes ahead we are?

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.840
We're many episodes ahead.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.220
Like 20? 20, yeah.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.220
For sure. 50? 100?

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.560
Yeah. But also, these are old games.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.520
They're not going to change their relevancy.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.520
No, I think it's like the window is closing for Stinger.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.540
In 2019, it wasn't an essential game, but it might be in 2020.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.580
You know? People are going to want to re-review on Mighty Bomb Jack.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:17.960
They're like, that wasn't fair.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.880
They recorded that episode in 2018.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:30.920
Anyway, guys, we're talking about Stinger, which is the second game in the long-running, quirky shooter series, Twin B.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.380
Now, did you guys know that this was a sequel?

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.740
No. I had no idea.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:41.060
Yeah. Well, it's good that you didn't know that because that sequel, I would have accused you of looking at Wikipedia had you have known that information.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:42.840
Don't we all, though? Like, shouldn't we?

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.440
I know. Well, that's the joke.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:02:05.220
But, yeah, Twin B is a series from Konami that started in the arcade, got a Famicom-only release for the first game, and then went under the Twin B name in Japan as well for this game in Japan for 1986 before coming to America.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:12.240
This is the only game in the entire Twin B series that comes to America, and it doesn't even get the name Twin B in it.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.240
Well, I think it's a cooler name.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.700
Like, it's more awesome.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.080
Stinger. And I think they picked the right game to port or to localize.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.220
Okay. I'd love to hear more about that later.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.440
Joe, any thoughts on this whole, like, Twin B thing?

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.980
I mean, not so much.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:36.500
I didn't play the original Twin B, but I'm interested to see what is different from it in this.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.980
But otherwise, going into it without knowing it's a sequel, it doesn't feel like a sequel.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:47.180
Yeah, this is the first time we're playing a sequel of any kind on the NES, and we don't even know that it's a sequel.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:55.180
Well, yeah, this game, they weren't going to throw us into the middle of some concluded, like, Hideo Kojima sort of thing.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:56.840
So I think we're okay.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:57.900
Konami Kojima.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:02:59.940
So it could happen.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:11.180
And also, you know, we have to talk about the fact that Konami made this game, and they're back at it with another shmup because we had Gradius from them, which we all unanimously loved.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:12.960
I still love Gradius.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.780
I haven't changed my feelings for that one at all.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:23.080
But maybe we could just talk a little bit, for those who didn't hear the Gradius episode at length, just maybe a little recap of why we liked Gradius so much.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:24.560
It had a lot of polish.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:31.140
It felt faster than, like, the console seemed to allow.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:40.020
It felt like not quite a next-gen game, but it felt like something more, not futuristic, but you know what I mean.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.220
Yeah, no, there was a lot more mechanics than we had been used to in a game.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:49.340
It was deeper. It had, like, a consistent tone throughout the whole game.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.840
It was just a fun game.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.080
Joe, anything to add?

00:03:52.340 --> 00:04:03.260
Yeah, I mean, I think just on top of it, like, looking polished, like, actually visually looking polished, it was also just, like, the gameplay mechanics were, I think, just deeper than anything we've played so far.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.140
I mean, I think I'm just kind of repeating what Sean said.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.240
No, and I think it also comes down to just player choice.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:13.080
Yeah. I think that, like, there was the first time ever where you could be like, oh, no, I'm going to do this instead.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:16.980
I'm going to save up for this power-up instead of this power-up.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:23.640
And, you know, I think that, you know, we all voted Gradius as an essential game, and I'm sure everybody's anxious to see how we're going to vote for Stinger.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:29.360
But before we talk about voting at all, we need to talk about the game at all.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.020
So let's get into our first impressions for Stinger.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.100
Who wants to go first?

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.020
I'll go first. All right.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:46.020
Joe, what do you got? Well, my very initial impression was that the art looked really clean and neat, but something about this art style, I think I've mentioned this before, makes me feel like it's a children's game, like a little kid's game.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:54.680
And I think it's just association with, I think we talked about, like, Leapfrog or one of those, like, one of those, like, handheld, like, preschool games that, like, you know what I'm talking about?

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.100
I remember that. Yeah, and it's got, like, that style or, like, some kind of browser game.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.040
But it doesn't feel like one of those games.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:09.300
But I think for somebody like myself that doesn't really, isn't a big fan of these kind of, like, shmups, I had a lot of fun with this.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.460
I thought this was, like, you know, jumping right into it.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:17.740
I was like, oh, this is more than what I thought it was going to be as just, like, a regular shoot-em-up.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:21.820
Yeah, and the childish thing, it doesn't help when, like, you're fighting, like, hangers.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.580
Right, yeah. Well, definitely it's, like, yeah.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:24.640
And the boss is a watermelon, you know?

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.840
It definitely kind of comes to, like, that childish thing is, like, sort of a quirky little style that they're deliberately doing.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.540
Oh, absolutely. And that kind of makes it fun.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.900
I think that's a twin bee thing in general, guys, just so that everybody knows.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:42.720
They were always going for a cartoon-like approach with that one as opposed to Gradius because they do have to be, like, two separate things, right?

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.560
You can't have, like, twin bee is just the horizontal version of Gradius.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:51.560
Like, or the vertical version of Gradius because that doesn't, you know, that's not enough.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.160
Sean, first impressions?

00:05:53.680 --> 00:06:03.120
When I looked ahead to see what we'd be playing this month, I saw Stinger and I, you know, I looked at the Wikipedia page and I looked at some screenshots.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:13.660
I'm like, great, another piece of shovelware that I'm just not going to care about and I'm going to play it for a half hour so I can, like, at least get a feel for how I'm going to shit on this game.

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:18.660
And then I played it and I didn't stop playing it for several hours.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:28.940
And I, again, like, we talked about this with Gradius, but I'm also not a fan of Shmups in general, but I think that there's just so much going on.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:39.260
Like, there are just, the mechanics are used in such a creative way that, like, you can just have fun with this for a long time.

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:47.560
And I think that with Gradius, like, the reputation sort of preceded it, whereas with Stinger, it just caught me completely by surprise.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:49.960
I'm going to echo those thoughts completely.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:56.020
I didn't know until I started up the game that this was a Konami thing, and I didn't know about the Twinbee thing either.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.440
I had heard Twinbee referenced a lot because I think it is big in Japan.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.680
We'll go into the whole franchise later in the show.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:03.780
We always cover that stuff.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:08.900
But I think, for me, what caught my eye was actually that cartoon stuff.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:16.240
Like, I started noticing, like, the enemies are actually, like, household objects or donuts or, like, you know, like, really weird things.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:18.500
And I think I was, like, enjoying myself.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:22.700
And then once I got to the first boss, I was like, oh, this is actually, like.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:23.580
It's like a bullet hell.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:27.460
Yeah, and also, like, it's a creative style for, like, a boss.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:28.580
It doesn't feel like.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:33.020
I don't feel as, you know, remember in Gradius, we always said that the death felt earned.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.060
But I definitely felt it was a challenging game.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:45.100
I think Stinger, right off the bat, felt too easy for me and then, like, was good about teaching me, like, how to get better at the game as each level got harder.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:46.020
Yeah, because it does get harder.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:47.300
It does get harder, absolutely.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:51.020
Okay, cool. So let's just talk real fast how to play.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:55.780
And I don't mean, like, actually, like, don't read me the manual or anything like that.

00:07:55.780 --> 00:08:03.800
But just a little bit about Stinger as a horizontal and vertical shmup because it does both styles.

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:08.820
You know, we've had 1942, which was vertical, and we've had Gradius, which was horizontal.

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:10.740
So this one kind of has a little bit of both.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:21.160
Yeah. I think that anybody that's played one or the other kind of shmup, vertical or horizontal, will immediately pick up on the controls of this game.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:26.380
It's not advanced or expert.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.560
Yeah. You just move and you shoot.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:41.020
Yeah. But your goal, as well as just, you know, shooting the enemies of whatever household object or, you know, or CDs or maybe there are records, I don't know.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:59.820
So your goal is also to get your power-ups through bells, which is an interesting mechanic where you sort of have to use this risk-reward decision-making of whether you want to keep that bell up in the air for a while or if you need to focus more about not getting shot.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:11.140
And, yeah, every level has, like, a very unique boss at the end, which sort of works differently depending on who you're fighting.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:15.540
It's not just some guy with more hit points or something along those lines.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:23.800
So, yeah, the whole difficulty curve and fun curve, it goes up.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:38.220
Yeah. And just to expand on the bell thing, which I thought was just, like, one of the most unique mechanics about this game, is that in order to get a power-up, you have to either shoot at these bells that fall out of clouds and every time you shoot at it, it bounces it up.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:41.460
And you basically just have to juggle it until it turns a certain color.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:53.740
And then it will give you a power-up based on the color it turns, which I thought was really interesting because, like you said, Sean, it's, like, a lot of times I found myself focusing too much on the bell and, like, oh, no, like, now there's, like, enemies that I'm not even paying attention to.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:56.960
Because I'm trying to juggle these bells and, like, get them to the right color.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:06.420
So it really adds, like, another layer of, like, if you want the power-ups, which if you get enough of these power-ups, like, you become a powerhouse in this game.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:07.560
It feels good. Yes.

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.700
So I thought that was, like, a really interesting way of doing it.

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:17.200
And even there's, like, even other, like, mechanics to help you juggle the, you know, other than just shooting it.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.960
Like, in the side scrolling, there's other ways to do it.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:21.040
Yeah. I actually used a lot.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:24.060
I don't know. You guys gave me a look like maybe you didn't use it so much.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.880
No, I used it. I used it quite a bit, too.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:28.960
You know, because there is two forms of attack here.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:30.060
One to attack.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.900
I think it's the B button lets you actually attack enemies that are on the screen.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:38.420
But then there's also, like, each world, each stage is, like, a different location.

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:42.880
I know there's, like, everybody loves to reference the pyramids in NES games for some reason.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:44.320
It's, like, Egypt is just the most amazing.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.200
I was surprised there were no Moai heads.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.780
Yeah, where were the Moai – there was Easter Island as a whole thing.

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:50.940
But we did go to, like, the universe.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:52.760
So at the end of the day.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:53.700
We went to the universe.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:56.060
Where were we before? It's described as the universe.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:01.540
I think that's very strange that it's, like, we weren't there before, but now we're there.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:08.040
But, yeah, you go to all these incredible places and, like, you know, the sphinxes are shooting at you from the ground.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:14.400
And so you have to use your secondary weapon, which is kind of like a lock-on material thing.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:20.200
It's like a bomb kind of thing, right? Yeah, like a bomb that has a very specific – like, it tells you where it's going to shoot as you shoot it.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.260
Yeah, specifically in the vertical portions.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:25.360
Right. Yes. Because in the horizontal, that button –

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:27.300
Is a heart that shoots upwards.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.080
Which really – I think it only helps you juggle the bells, right?

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:36.620
It's only for bells, yeah. But I use that a lot because then you could switch between – you could be shooting things in front of you and keeping a bell right above you.

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:38.960
It makes it a little easier to multitask.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.700
Juggling is, I think, one of the key words in this game.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:47.440
Yes. But, you know, yeah, it's very obvious, like both Sean and Joe have said.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:56.180
If you know shmups, you know that this is essentially just clearing waves of enemies as you scroll through the stage and then finally being greeted by a boss before going to the next stage.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:59.580
That's not to say that it's too simple or too repetitive.

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:00.520
We'll get into that.

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:04.620
But I just – I think that that's a general gist of how to play Stinger.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:08.580
There you go. All right, guys.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:11.380
So let's do a little bit of the manual here.

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:17.860
And I really just want to focus in on the story because I think that the story in this one – funny enough, it was changed.

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.160
Okay? This is all American only.

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:24.500
If you play the Japanese version, it's not really – it's a much more advanced story.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:29.660
There's actually a cut scene where three fighter pilots, like, talk to each other and explain –

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:33.420
In the Japanese one. And that was removed from this one.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:40.640
Because the American version is basically just a simplified aerial version of scoon where they just want to eat us.

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:45.520
But instead of eating man meat, they eat – they just turn us into tasty treats.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:47.100
Yeah. Cotton candy, right?

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:51.080
Yeah, cotton candy. But, you know, in the opening cut scene for the game –

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:51.900
I don't know what's happening.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.420
The aliens go to the Konami headquarters.

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:55.640
Is that Konami?

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.360
It says Konami at the sign.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.720
In the image there's a sign that's like – that says Konami.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:03.820
That's actually like a part of the picture.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:10.920
And the craziest part is that – okay, so they're attacking the Konami headquarters, which is new for this American version, right?

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:15.000
But then also, why does the Konami headquarters look so alien?

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:18.980
Yeah, it looks like a flying saucer itself, and then a flying saucer comes down and attacks them.

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:20.140
Yeah, I didn't know what I was looking at.

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:23.220
I just know that, like, a weird-looking dude got kidnapped.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:24.720
Konami's top scientist.

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:27.600
Konami's top scientist, which I talked to Joe about this before the show.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:33.780
I think it's so funny that, like, a scientist works at a video game headquarters, like, as if they make the games with test tubes.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.520
You know, they're like, hmm, let's find the right amount of gameplay balance.

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:39.640
The fun chemical.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.240
But they do steal – his name is Dr.

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.800
Cinnamon, according to the manual.

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.000
So I think that that's pretty sexy.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:49.400
You've got to rescue Dr.

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:53.380
Cinnamon from a watermelon and a saxophone.

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:55.420
Yeah. Yeah, it's so great.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:57.960
People are looking this up right now to make sure we're not just making it up.

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:01.480
Like, if this posts on April 1st or something, I know it says something else, but it's a saxophone.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:09.220
I would highly recommend, like, the YouTube version of this one because you would get to see all the crazy stuff we're talking about as we talk about it.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:17.020
And then also, one more thing about, like, the whole, like, the plot and everything like that is, you know, that's it.

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:24.420
Like, a scientist has been kidnapped, and now we have to travel throughout the entire universe just to get him back?

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:25.240
What did he have?

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:32.200
It is nice, though, that it's not, like, a princess or your girlfriend, which has been done, like, a trillion times since, like, these games.

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:33.420
The beginning of time. Since we started playing.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:37.900
Yeah, but since we started playing even these NES games, like, every game is, like, the same thing.

00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:39.840
If I recall. So it's so different now.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:45.740
If I recall correctly, I believe that they were trying to zap a recipe out of his head.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:47.000
Okay, that would make sense.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:48.220
He's Dr. Cinnamon. Yeah.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:51.860
To, you know, to turn the earth into cotton candy.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:52.900
Why does he have that recipe?

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:55.180
I don't know. But it's in his head, and they need to zap it out.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:56.580
Right, of course. That's the word they used.

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:01.440
Yeah, I think I have here just a little, they're called the attack-ons, or the aliens.

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:09.260
And they're scheming to zap the professor's brain, retrieving his secret bio-nuclear sweetener formula.

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:13.580
There it is. So it's a little, it's a little Gradius meets Burger Time.

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:22.640
I think a lot of the localization teams in these early NES games just had to be, you know, having, just having the time of their lives.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:26.340
Just like, I'm gonna, I'm gonna call them the attack-ons.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:30.920
Yeah. And the bio-nuclear something shit, man.

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:33.180
Yeah, yeah. Oh my gosh.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:37.860
Yeah, it's true. I think it's funny that the Japanese ones had a totally different story, and they were like, screw it.

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:40.000
Yeah. Let them do what they need to do.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:41.840
There's gotta be a reason hangers are in this game.

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:44.900
Okay. That's the story.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:46.500
We're not gonna do any more on that.

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:26.700
They come to, okay, they come to listen to this for deep lore.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:29.500
Yeah. We always deliver on the deep lore, don't we?

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:31.160
I always wanna know what's happening.

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:35.820
And speaking of happening, you know, they don't waste any time.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:41.180
The gameplay goes right into the horizontal shmup is the first one.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:50.640
And what's interesting about that, and this is where we get into the whole Twinbee thing, is that the first game, Twinbee had no horizontal stages whatsoever.

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:53.860
Twinbee was a vertical shmup through and through.

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:56.220
That's what made the difference between Gradius.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:17:06.740
Now you have, you know, this horizontal stage where you have to juggle the bells in front of you while also, you know, attacking the enemies.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:11.980
But it doesn't feel as polished to me as the vertical stages.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:16.480
Oh, I like the horizontal stages more.

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:18.100
Oh, you like the horizontal stages more?

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:20.200
Yeah. Okay. We'll talk about that a little later.

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:22.260
Let's just talk about the horizontal stages in general.

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:27.460
Since you like them, maybe you tell me a little bit about, you know, what was it?

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.120
What was pleasing about going through the horizontal stages?

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:32.960
Well, I think that the horizontal stages have a...

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:39.900
One thing I will say about this game in general is it doesn't have as much polish as Gradius.

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.100
And this is gonna come up at any time.

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:43.040
You might as well say it now.

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:44.280
So I may as well say it now.

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:46.580
Like, this is a more...

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:48.340
It's not more jank.

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:57.540
It just looks more sketched, more like a sketch instead of like a finished piece than Gradius.

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:05.140
But one thing I'll say is that I did like the art more in the side-scrolling portions where there's like a horizon.

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:08.760
And, you know, it just was more pleasing to the eye for me.

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:10.680
You said in Gradius?

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:18.860
I'm comparing the sort of art style within Stinger of the side-scrolling portions to the vertical-scrolling portions.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:23.840
But yeah, the fact that you get to see like the pyramids and the side-scrolling ones.

00:18:24.040 --> 00:18:34.100
And again, for some reason, every city in these NES games is just littered with gigantic crosses in the cityscape.

00:18:34.100 --> 00:18:42.120
But yeah, I just like that more than the weird blobs on the ground in the vertical-scrolling portions.

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:48.760
Got it. And in terms of like fighting enemies, did you find it easier on the horizontal stages than the vertical stages?

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:50.760
Was it more fun?

00:18:50.980 --> 00:19:02.100
I think that since just by virtue of the fact that you're on a 4x3 television, you have more space to see where the enemies are coming from.

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:06.980
When you're scrolling horizontally than in the vertical-scrolling ones.

00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:13.760
They sort of, it seems to travel faster when you're going up as opposed to going right.

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:18.860
See, I liked attacking the enemies on the horizontal one for sure.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:26.060
You had more time to kind of like, you know, in these games you're always anticipating where your enemy is going to be, not where it currently is.

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:35.240
And I felt like I had more time to kind of think about that in the horizontal one because they were always coming pretty much, with the exception of the final stage, they're always coming from the right.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:39.000
So you always have a chance to kind of like think about your attack.

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:46.580
Whereas in the vertical stages, you have like sometimes they come from like the bottom of the, you know, they always come from the left and right.

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:48.640
But they come from the bottom or they come from top down.

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:51.740
It's like there was a lot more ambush going on.

00:19:51.820 --> 00:20:01.480
Yeah. But I would say that if the main idea of this game is that you're trying to get through waves of enemies while also balancing this power-up mechanic,

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:09.640
I thought that that was a lot more fleshed out and done better in the vertical stages than the horizontal stages.

00:20:09.860 --> 00:20:14.300
It just didn't feel like, especially knowing that Twinbee originated as a vertical thing,

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:17.840
it felt like the horizontal stages were just kind of added as an afterthought.

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:24.660
Like, this will surely work using the exact same mechanics and rules as the vertical stages without any changes.

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:29.580
Interesting. I can see why you think that, like why you feel that way, because it does feel like there's,

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:35.980
and I don't know if I necessarily say it felt like to me there was more to the vertical stages.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:40.120
But it just, to me, it felt like it was a good difference between the two of them.

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.040
Like I kind of liked, I don't know if I have a preference.

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:45.900
I just liked that it switched up to like, oh, now you've got to think about the things on the ground,

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:49.000
or now you have to think about using those hearts to your advantage.

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:53.440
Like it was just like enough of a difference between it, regardless of whether you're going vertically or horizontally.

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:54.800
It keeps it from getting stale. Exactly.

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:56.660
Yeah. I'm not a Twinbee purist.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:59.640
I'm not saying that like, oh, they should have only had vertical stages.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:01.880
I too enjoyed like going back between them.

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:03.540
And it's not like it's ABAB.

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:07.460
Like sometimes it's like two horizontal stages and then a vertical stage.

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:09.420
So I, you know, I didn't mind it.

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:17.440
I think personally that the layout of the game and the rules of the game worked better in the vertical stages than the horizontal one.

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.520
That's my only, I guess, like note on that.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:26.620
Okay. And then, you know, let's talk about the bells because that's pretty much like how you get the power-ups.

00:21:27.080 --> 00:21:33.240
That change of like, the change in colors, it is set on the vertical stages.

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:35.780
But on the horizontal stages, it's not set to a number.

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:42.140
So on the, at least with me, I might be going crazy here, but I felt like I should bring this up on the show.

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:45.900
In the vertical stages, every five, it'll change color.

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:47.840
So hit it five times, it changes to blue.

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:49.980
Hit it five more times, it changes to white.

00:21:50.260 --> 00:21:52.880
Hit it five more times, it changes to red, so on and so forth.

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:57.660
When you go to the horizontal stages, it seemed random.

00:21:58.160 --> 00:21:59.380
It seemed like. Yeah, I think it was.

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:02.840
And it also doesn't determine, like, you don't know which color is going to show up next.

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:18.320
I think because that you're always, I feel like just because you're always going to be shooting under a bell, if you've hit a cloud, they had to make it a little more difficult for you to get something good.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:25.220
Because I was, when I first started the game, I didn't read the manual before I started, so I didn't know what the bells were doing.

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:27.920
I was like, oh, I guess that just popped up.

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:29.560
That gives you points. Easy 500 points.

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:30.860
That's what I thought, too.

00:22:31.700 --> 00:22:36.560
Because you're just going to keep incidentally shooting these bells, maybe that was, like, a balance thing.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:48.180
And I think another thing that makes it a little more difficult is that we haven't really mentioned that when you hit it however many times and it turns blue, it's only blue for that.

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:50.580
If you hit it again, it'll turn back to whatever, yellow.

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:52.260
It's the original color where it gives you nothing.

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:53.980
So, like, you can't just spam it.

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:57.840
You have to, like, notice, like, it turns blue, stop shooting it immediately because your next one is going to go away.

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:05.660
And if this doesn't, like, sound like anything right now, basically, if you, there are these bells that will fall out of, like, you know, the scenery.

00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:13.300
And then if you shoot them, they sort of do that hop slash pop that the cans do in Duck Hunt.

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:15.840
I feel like it's a very similar.

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:18.740
No, I'm not Duck Hunt.

00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:20.440
It was Hogan's Alley.

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:21.300
Hogan's Alley. That's right.

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:22.500
Yeah, that's what I meant.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:26.080
But it felt very similar to that.

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:27.140
I love a good Duck Hunt reference.

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:28.860
Even if it's wrong.

00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:33.540
Yeah. So that's what we mean by, like, trying to juggle these things.

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:43.260
Yeah. And I think that's a unique mechanic, especially the color changing thing because there's the risk reward that you guys were talking about between, like, it's blue now and that really doesn't do too much for me.

00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:44.500
I'd rather have the white bell.

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:49.360
But if I hit the blue bell again, I have to wait for the cycle to change again.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:54.180
So I might just be stuck with 500 points because it's not like you can just easily juggle those things.

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:58.880
Anybody who's played this game knows that you're going to drop a lot of them.

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:02.300
Yeah. And then sometimes more come on screen, which you think would be good because you could drop one.

00:24:02.380 --> 00:24:04.840
But then you lose track of, like, wait, which one did I hit 10 times?

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:06.480
It's like when you get the multi-ball and break out.

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:07.880
Yes, exactly. Yeah.

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:10.040
You think it's a good thing that now you have three balls.

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:11.680
But you don't.

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:13.640
You just don't watch any of them.

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:25.240
Exactly. And I just wanted to talk about the enemies for a hot minute here because they start off, like, we talked about there's some donuts and, like, some fruit and stuff at the beginning.

00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:28.700
The second stage has, like, normal stuff like turtles and jellyfish.

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:29.580
That's normal, I guess.

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:30.600
Yeah. And then, like, no.

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:32.740
But then the third stage, it just gets out of control.

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:38.040
There's a shoe that chomps on you and there's a pair of scissors.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:41.020
But, like, those, like, scissors that you use outside.

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:42.460
Scissors are in. The hedge clippers.

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:45.460
Scissors or hedge clippers are inherently intimidating.

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:50.540
So. And then my favorites, hanger, which is just a hanger.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:55.840
And then also, like, what I have to imagine is a shower head with water actually coming out of it.

00:24:56.480 --> 00:24:58.080
Yeah. You know?

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:00.680
Like, these things are just getting ridiculous.

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:05.060
It kind of reminded me a little bit of the feeling I had for Kung Fu.

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:06.900
The first level was, like, people.

00:25:07.060 --> 00:25:09.500
The second level were, like, dragons and snakes.

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:11.140
Plants. Yeah, plants. Yeah, pots.

00:25:11.460 --> 00:25:12.300
Yeah. Yep.

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:17.060
And it doesn't even end there, too, because then you get into, like, stage four has, like, TVs.

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:20.060
Yeah. And telephones and iron.

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:27.580
This game is all about the developer's fear of technology and just domestic living.

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:29.760
I think that's what he was trying to say.

00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:30.700
Yeah. That's obvious.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:32.840
And how we're being controlled by our products.

00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:40.900
Exactly. And, you know, just a shout-out to the name of all of these stages, because nobody would ever know them, but since I'm looking at the manual here.

00:25:41.060 --> 00:25:44.460
We have the Donbury Island, which is the first stage.

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:48.460
Then the Lama Yadora Kingdom for the stage two.

00:25:49.180 --> 00:25:53.800
And then what you think is Egypt is actually the Aldorado Province.

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:57.640
Aldorado? The Aldorado Province.

00:25:58.220 --> 00:25:59.480
Like a Chevy Aldorado?

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:00.520
Yeah, I guess so.

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:03.900
Because then from there, it just starts to get nonsensical.

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:07.440
It's the Erururu Fusuhuku country.

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:08.820
Just country.

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:11.800
Country, yes. Which is also just an ice country.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:14.920
But this is, like, the whole idea of, like, you're not on Earth.

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:18.860
Like, you're traveling to all these crazy planets to retrieve Dr.

00:26:18.860 --> 00:26:20.480
Cinnamon. No, it's all on the same planet.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:22.240
You're on the same planet.

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:24.440
Oh, yeah, that's true. And you leave the space to the universe.

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:27.640
Yeah. And then the Zamabi Kingdom.

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:33.240
And then the last one is literally just LPU equals and then an upside down L.

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:35.720
Otherwise known as the Gamma Empire.

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:39.000
But I kid you not, they actually, like, do that.

00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:41.040
Yeah. It's in parentheses, Gamma.

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:43.000
Like, it must be their language.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:44.880
Empire. And I love it.

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:46.040
I think that that's so funny.

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:47.820
And then stage seven, of course, the universe.

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:51.420
Yes. But we got ahead of ourselves.

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:53.740
You know, we were talking about the power-ups there for a minute.

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:55.140
I'd love to just kind of get back to that.

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:57.600
Because you guys were talking about being a powerhouse in this game.

00:26:57.980 --> 00:26:59.180
And I couldn't agree more.

00:26:59.320 --> 00:27:00.740
Once you get that triple shot.

00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:02.980
Yeah. It spreads out between the three different ways.

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:04.500
Triple shot, then following with ghosts.

00:27:04.760 --> 00:27:06.740
Yeah. And if you have the long shot and the triple shot.

00:27:06.820 --> 00:27:08.360
The long shot, which just shoots through everything.

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:10.660
Oh, the laser. Yeah. I just call it whatever.

00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:15.040
What's funny about the laser, too, is, like, I'd prefer the triple shot throughout most

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:16.600
of the, you know, because of that spread.

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:19.560
It attacks everything above, in the center, and below you.

00:27:19.660 --> 00:27:25.940
And I'd prefer that for, like, the normal, like, wave of enemies.

00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:29.060
But then, like, the laser is so good for the bosses.

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:33.220
Yeah. Well, I didn't. I like to laser when you have, because you can have two, yeah, ghost ships, I guess.

00:27:33.400 --> 00:27:34.440
Two other ships following you.

00:27:34.540 --> 00:27:37.920
Yep. When you have the laser for that, then it's, like, yeah, it's not as much as if you had

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:38.800
three spread shots.

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:40.040
But there's just three lasers.

00:27:40.040 --> 00:27:41.180
Yeah. And it just.

00:27:41.300 --> 00:27:46.180
I do want to mention, like, a shout-out, I guess, for getting those ghosts, because

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:47.680
that takes a lot of hits on a bell.

00:27:47.740 --> 00:27:48.980
Oh, I didn't get the ghosts.

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:50.120
I watched somebody get the ghosts.

00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:54.040
Oh, okay. I was going to say. I got the ghosts on the first level and carried it a ways, but then I never was able

00:27:54.040 --> 00:27:56.760
to get it again. That's probably the best way to do it, because the first level is so relaxed.

00:27:56.860 --> 00:27:58.380
Right. I would say too easy.

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:02.160
It actually took me several playthroughs just to realize how the bells worked, because

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:03.720
I didn't have any power-ups for a while.

00:28:03.800 --> 00:28:08.340
Yeah, I made it to the fifth level without knowing what the bells did.

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:11.820
And then I learned what the bells did, and I was like, I got to go back to the beginning.

00:28:11.920 --> 00:28:13.120
I've been playing on hard mode.

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:14.740
