00:30:15.440 --> 00:30:17.660
But then when I went back with the power-ups, it was like three hits.

00:30:17.740 --> 00:30:18.820
It was almost like two months.

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:20.840
Yeah, the laser will take him out easily.

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:26.100
And you can also shoot down his tentacles, I guess that's what those are.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:27.360
You can shoot those down.

00:30:27.620 --> 00:30:31.200
So you can essentially just block his attacks and then shoot him when he's open.

00:30:31.520 --> 00:30:32.620
Yeah. So I thought that was weird.

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:37.220
Easy peasy. Fang is really tough because he only has an opening on his mouth.

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:40.160
Yeah, and the bubbles can become a bit of a headache.

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:42.520
Is that the one where there's like the rotating line of bubbles?

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:43.840
Or no, is that the next one? It's the pink bubbles.

00:30:44.400 --> 00:30:45.540
That's an easier one for me.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:46.880
Yeah, the pink bubbles.

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:49.220
And then who do we have next?

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:52.280
Oh, Luther. That's the guy with the rotating thing.

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:54.960
So did you guys, for that one, did you guys hide in the corner?

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:56.500
No. I just went around in a circle.

00:30:56.500 --> 00:30:58.040
That's what I did. I was going around in a circle.

00:30:58.120 --> 00:31:00.740
But then when I was watching a playthrough later, the guy just went in the corner and

00:31:00.740 --> 00:31:02.740
we were untouchable, which I didn't think was possible.

00:31:03.180 --> 00:31:04.060
I didn't think so either.

00:31:04.240 --> 00:31:05.060
You don't need to do that.

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:09.860
It was fun to kind of, you shoot while you can and then the sword or whatever the heck it

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:14.260
is comes around and you have to kind of race it around again and have time to shoot.

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:15.460
I feel like that would have been better.

00:31:15.480 --> 00:31:16.440
You just take the inside track.

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:18.080
Yeah. Easy peasy. Exactly.

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:22.560
But it's like you have some time to shoot.

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:24.360
The thing comes around, you got to race it around.

00:31:24.360 --> 00:31:27.320
I did test my luck a bit trying to outrun that guy.

00:31:27.520 --> 00:31:30.460
I like how I said, and then we have Luther and everybody was like, yeah, Luther.

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:32.340
Luther, the robot head.

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:34.420
Yeah. I don't know what he's supposed to be.

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:35.800
I'm going to say he's supposed to be a computer.

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:42.480
But he also looks like he's measuring amplitude on stuff.

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:43.500
So like, who knows?

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:47.020
Because then you have Master Blaster, who's actually just a boombox.

00:31:47.260 --> 00:31:51.520
Yeah. And I didn't hate or like that one.

00:31:51.620 --> 00:31:54.620
I thought that was actually like the least creative one out of the batch.

00:31:55.300 --> 00:31:56.500
You have to hit him in the speakers.

00:31:56.740 --> 00:31:57.940
Yeah. You have to hit him in the speakers.

00:31:58.340 --> 00:31:59.560
Where it hurts, of course.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:04.600
Exactly. And then Solar B Three Eyes, which was that sun.

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:06.060
And I thought that was so random.

00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:08.780
It's like we had so many cool bosses and now we have the sun.

00:32:08.780 --> 00:32:10.680
But he's completely avoidable.

00:32:11.180 --> 00:32:12.980
Yeah. He just goes up and down.

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:18.660
You can always get his little projectiles if we just move over to the left while he's farther away.

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:20.400
And then you come back and shoot him in the face.

00:32:20.540 --> 00:32:25.360
I thought he should have done something a little more dangerous.

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:28.580
Like move around in a circle like the other guy did.

00:32:28.640 --> 00:32:29.820
Or Luther.

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:32.980
I thought something should have happened there.

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:37.780
It was disappointing that that stage is so hard and he's just a pretty easy boss at the end of it.

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:42.700
And I think it's just a theme in general for this game is that the bosses are pretty easy, but I like them all.

00:32:42.880 --> 00:32:44.160
Yeah. They're all very well designed.

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:47.620
They're unique. They make you play the game differently.

00:32:48.680 --> 00:32:55.140
And last but not least, we have the attack on Emperor who is like a dragon.

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:56.120
A disguised dragon.

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:57.680
Yeah. He's like a – yeah.

00:32:57.680 --> 00:33:01.140
He's a dragon who's essentially like his power is homing attack.

00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:03.660
He just reaches out for you.

00:33:03.780 --> 00:33:04.980
He tries to eat the ship alive.

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:09.640
I honestly – I beat him after a very long time.

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:17.980
I couldn't get through this guy for a very long time because I couldn't – I didn't know like at what point do I turn.

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:20.700
Or like I kept making this S shape and kept getting eaten.

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:22.640
Like how did you guys fare with him?

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:27.080
I think what was interesting was I didn't realize that he was homing at me at first.

00:33:27.080 --> 00:33:29.980
I think that took like a death and a half to realize.

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:37.580
And then I also after finding that out, all of a sudden I was like, but I'm never going to be able to shoot him unless he comes back to the right side.

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:43.640
For those who have a hard time imagining it, you have this snake-like creature who comes in from the right side.

00:33:43.740 --> 00:33:45.000
You have to aim for his head.

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:46.240
You get maybe a few shots.

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:48.960
And then he's like on the screen just kind of chasing you.

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:52.120
80% of the time he's going to be behind the direction that you're shooting.

00:33:52.120 --> 00:34:04.720
So the only way to really get about him is to kind of start in the right and then drift over to the left, trick him so that he goes to the left side of the screen and then comes out the other end, the right end.

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:09.760
Yeah. And so I think that it's not an incredibly hard final boss by any means.

00:34:10.100 --> 00:34:11.460
That's hard for me. No, no, no.

00:34:11.460 --> 00:34:15.300
Meaning like it just takes a lot of patience, a lot of strategy.

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:18.760
Something that like I don't think that we have necessarily.

00:34:19.660 --> 00:34:20.460
No strategy.

00:34:20.700 --> 00:34:23.040
A lot of times I like to just rush in on things.

00:34:23.220 --> 00:34:25.440
I'm a big fan of press X to win.

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:34.820
I love to just like just beat things to a pulp and not really think about like, oh, and my defensive strategy is this and I'm going to equip these things because I need this.

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:37.480
It's like, no, I'm just going to attack him until he dies.

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:38.780
You must be really bad at smash.

00:34:38.780 --> 00:34:41.180
Smash. Excuse you.

00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:44.180
I don't have strategy, Mr.

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:45.220
Mike. Yeah, I don't know.

00:34:45.280 --> 00:34:46.380
I think I'm pretty good at Smash Brothers.

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:50.740
Anyway, I think that that was like for me, that was the thing.

00:34:50.840 --> 00:34:52.680
But I was able to beat him. So I'm pretty proud of myself.

00:34:52.820 --> 00:34:54.200
Yeah. Joe?

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:56.120
Well, like I said, I only got to level five.

00:34:56.420 --> 00:35:02.360
Okay. But when after when I watched some like footage of it, I kind of realized like, oh, I was actually I was pretty close to the end.

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:05.480
I just figured there was a lot more to it, which is why I stopped.

00:35:05.720 --> 00:35:08.080
But so I didn't face him.

00:35:08.080 --> 00:35:09.580
So I don't know what it's like to face him.

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:16.240
But I do want to say that even though I didn't move on, you get continues in this game.

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:20.880
So you you can take your time and you can die and keep going.

00:35:21.020 --> 00:35:23.580
And whatever. I feel like a lot of games haven't done that for us yet.

00:35:23.740 --> 00:35:25.700
So remember when that used to be a big thing for us?

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:27.660
I mean, like, we're you know, we're like finally continues.

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:30.180
Yeah. We're so many and we're in so many episodes now.

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:35.280
But early on, that was like a make or break feature for a game was like, where does it let you continue from?

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:36.580
Does it start you over from the very beginning?

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:40.220
And now it's like we're barely like we're how many minutes are we into this episode?

00:35:40.360 --> 00:35:41.780
And we're just finally talking about it.

00:35:41.820 --> 00:35:45.400
Right. But I still think I still think we're going to be playing a lot that don't have continues.

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:46.820
Absolutely. It's going to be frustrating.

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:51.900
Yeah. But I thought this was yeah, that was great because, you know, I'm not going to pretend like I did it all in one shot.

00:35:52.140 --> 00:35:55.500
I abused continues like crazy.

00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:59.580
And I also, you know, if I'm being honest, I abused the juggling system, too.

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:03.400
I really I really made sure that I was getting the best power ups as soon as I could.

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:08.000
And I didn't mind dying because I knew that I would just be able to continue right from where I left off.

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:10.760
You need the best power ups to continue moving on.

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:15.380
Because it is devastating, especially if you kind of do what I did where in level one you get all the power ups.

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:18.880
And it's it's not too hard to keep those power ups for the first couple of levels.

00:36:19.020 --> 00:36:26.120
But then once you first lose those power ups, if you're in like level three or four and suddenly you're back to for the first time, you're back to your base level.

00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:30.220
It's it's devastating to be like one thing I'd like to mention, though, about death.

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:33.240
Oh, this was going to be the next thing that we were going to talk about.

00:36:33.380 --> 00:36:34.320
So I'm glad you're bringing it up.

00:36:34.460 --> 00:36:44.120
Yeah. One thing that we gave Gradius flack for was that as soon as you die and you're with all of your powers like you better just reset the game.

00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:48.160
Because you're not going to be able to get past like level four with your little pea shooter.

00:36:49.300 --> 00:36:57.260
But with this game, you do have a recovery mechanic that when you die, you can go and pick up your ghost and get your stuff back.

00:36:57.500 --> 00:36:59.680
Yeah, it's it's actually like the soul.

00:36:59.960 --> 00:37:01.280
It is dark souls.

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:05.540
It's dark souls like but also way before dark souls came up with the idea.

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:07.460
It's like, no, I just I love this.

00:37:07.460 --> 00:37:09.540
It's a little ghost ship.

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:12.360
You know, if you die at the very top of the screen, it's gone.

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:16.320
Oh, yeah. But if you die at the bottom of the screen, you have a good chance to like re catch it.

00:37:16.480 --> 00:37:24.420
And then you can basically like dodge the enemies, dodge everything else, collect your soul, and then you get all your power ups back.

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:29.940
And then what's really neat is if you did collect it, the next time you die, you won't be able to get the soul again.

00:37:30.100 --> 00:37:31.780
It's like a one catch thing.

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:33.340
It's not in a system you can abuse.

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:41.020
But I do like that if you die and you don't catch it and then you die again, you have a chance at the new soul if it has any power ups.

00:37:41.180 --> 00:37:48.620
Yeah. It's just a really neat thing in a game that I, you know, that now that's like a feature that I was like, oh, why didn't Gradius have that?

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:57.260
Exactly. You know? Like, yeah, it's very like to that universe, that cartoon style like silliness of like a soul to your ship.

00:37:57.560 --> 00:38:00.680
But I just I was really blown away by that.

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:03.300
And I'm kind of surprised it took us this long to talk about it.

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:04.320
But I'm glad we got it in there.

00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:10.100
A lot of things about this game, this might be a little going on a tangent, but a lot of things about this game oddly remind me of Cuphead.

00:38:10.660 --> 00:38:16.380
I mean, the weird bosses, both in design and in like the way you fight them.

00:38:16.500 --> 00:38:17.560
And this I didn't even think of that.

00:38:17.660 --> 00:38:24.420
But, yeah, the soul thing when you're playing two player Cuphead, same thing happens where your partner has to grab your soul to like bring you back down.

00:38:24.640 --> 00:38:29.940
Absolutely. There's a lot of that's just like as we're going, I'm like finding more and more things that like make that remind me of it.

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:31.460
And it is it is a cartoony.

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:32.760
Yeah, the art style in general.

00:38:33.040 --> 00:39:07.900
Yeah. Thank you.

00:40:03.300 --> 00:40:07.700
They had a word for it in the manual for when you shoot from the same spot on the screen.

00:40:08.080 --> 00:40:09.420
It's like, oh, a ripple gun.

00:40:10.840 --> 00:40:12.560
You know, we did see that.

00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:16.820
What was it? Because we had a broken controller, so we didn't know how to get very far in it.

00:40:16.820 --> 00:40:20.980
Well, but are you talking about it's like a special attack only for two players?

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:25.900
Yeah, I think if you combine your strength and go into the same spot on the screen.

00:40:25.900 --> 00:40:26.920
It was a big explosion.

00:40:27.160 --> 00:40:28.060
Is that the ripple attack?

00:40:28.060 --> 00:40:29.160
Yeah, I guess that's what it was.

00:40:29.280 --> 00:40:31.300
And it cleared out a whole wave of enemies.

00:40:31.420 --> 00:40:32.480
That's really cool to see.

00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:35.260
Remember I said, what was that?

00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:39.000
Yeah. Because we played for like 10 seconds and then the controller wasn't working.

00:40:39.100 --> 00:40:42.460
But I do remember I was just more focused on the fact that I couldn't move.

00:40:42.620 --> 00:40:45.220
Yeah. And also that was a very stylized attack, too.

00:40:45.340 --> 00:40:49.400
It was like this yellow, like long rectangle thing that showed up on the screen.

00:40:49.500 --> 00:40:54.940
And I was like, oh. So yeah, the longer we talk, the more little goodies come out of this very simple system.

00:40:54.940 --> 00:41:03.620
Yep. And I would be, I think it now would be a great time to also just mention that in the Japanese version, this was a three player game.

00:41:03.740 --> 00:41:05.000
I did read about that.

00:41:05.200 --> 00:41:06.480
That is awesome. Come on.

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:07.600
What do you do with the third?

00:41:07.720 --> 00:41:09.460
The third player is also a ship.

00:41:09.620 --> 00:41:11.740
Just a third ship. All three of them on the screen at the same time.

00:41:11.900 --> 00:41:18.100
And now that's crazy because the Famicom just got the like accessory that you add on to the add four.

00:41:18.280 --> 00:41:21.100
Yeah, the multi-tab to add four controllers.

00:41:21.100 --> 00:41:30.020
And they managed to program three ships into, and that's why that cut scene has three fighters talking to each other because you can have up to three.

00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:33.020
You can be those people. But obviously the levels don't get any harder, right?

00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:34.220
The levels don't get any harder.

00:41:35.320 --> 00:41:40.660
One thing that they took out of the American version of the game was a difficulty select.

00:41:40.860 --> 00:41:45.780
You start on normal in the American version, and then once you beat the game, you unlock the hard mode.

00:41:45.780 --> 00:41:52.100
It's immediately like the stage eight because there's seven stages, and then it just goes right to the next stage, which is the first one again.

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:53.760
But it's now on hard mode.

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:57.420
The Japanese one did let you choose between normal and hard.

00:41:57.780 --> 00:41:59.660
And I mean, that's pretty much Stinger in a nutshell.

00:42:00.040 --> 00:42:04.400
So I kind of, you know, we've been talking so much about Gradius throughout this game.

00:42:04.460 --> 00:42:13.900
Maybe we could just talk about like the differences and difficulty between this and Gradius because, you know, we love Gradius, but we didn't beat that one.

00:42:13.900 --> 00:42:20.640
But we were able to kind of get our way through Stinger one way or another, no matter how many continues it took.

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:23.220
So what does that say, guys, about these two games?

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:30.380
I don't know. I mean, Gradius was definitely more difficult, but I think that it was, for the most part, welcome difficulty.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:35.620
Like I thought some of Stinger was maybe a little too easy, at least with all the power-ups at the beginning.

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:37.000
I can't remember.

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:39.100
In Gradius, you don't get continues, right?

00:42:39.740 --> 00:42:40.920
No, you get continues.

00:42:40.920 --> 00:42:46.780
Okay, then I think that most of the difficulty in Gradius was not frustrating, if I remember correctly.

00:42:47.120 --> 00:42:49.660
I think this could have benefited from a little more difficulty.

00:42:50.280 --> 00:43:00.480
I think that the fact that both these games have continues makes both of their inherent difficulty easier to swallow.

00:43:00.600 --> 00:43:06.920
Because even this game, we keep saying that it's easy, but that's only in comparison to Gradius.

00:43:07.100 --> 00:43:10.140
Right, yeah, definitely. It definitely would be a lot harder if you couldn't continue.

00:43:10.280 --> 00:43:13.460
Yeah, these games would be maddening if you couldn't continue.

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:24.440
But I think that, you know, I think that they're not equal, but they both have enough difficulty for it to be a challenge and fun at the same time.

00:43:25.060 --> 00:43:27.620
Yeah, and I don't really have much to add there.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:34.540
I definitely think that these two games are held together because it's both Konami.

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:36.060
It's both around that same time.

00:43:36.300 --> 00:43:40.000
It's kind of like, for the NES at least, it's our standard for Shmup.

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:44.360
You know, Capcom came out with 1942, but we didn't necessarily love that one.

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:52.200
And I think maybe that's because it had something to do with being like trying to ground itself more in reality while these had like fun with the idea.

00:43:52.340 --> 00:43:55.820
I hated 1942. Okay, that's totally fine to hate on 1942.

00:43:55.820 --> 00:44:03.680
But I think if you're asking me to choose between the two, I think thankfully there's enough difference between them.

00:44:04.360 --> 00:44:13.800
And that's impressive from a developer standpoint too, that it's not like Konami made Gradius and then Data East comes out with Stinger.

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:25.440
You know, like it's interesting that the same company is able to make two games that are pretty different from each other in the respect that I would say like, well, some people are going to like Gradius more and some people are going to like Stinger more.

00:44:25.800 --> 00:44:34.820
You know, you're definitely going to need to be a fan on some level of like the idea of like a shooter that gets like punishingly hard.

00:44:34.960 --> 00:44:39.420
Yeah. Towards the end of it, because that puts off a lot of people, those bullet hell games.

00:44:39.420 --> 00:44:44.040
And I don't think that either one of these becomes like the level of like Ikaruga does today.

00:44:44.040 --> 00:44:45.740
No, those are parodies of themselves.

00:44:45.940 --> 00:44:53.580
Yeah, exactly. But I definitely think like later stages will kind of make you yearn for stage one.

00:44:53.720 --> 00:44:56.080
The one that we've been talking about, like why is stage one so easy?

00:44:56.220 --> 00:44:57.820
It's like just chill, man.

00:44:58.960 --> 00:45:00.620
Isn't it nice that you get a nice break?

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:04.300
Yeah. And that's all I have to say about the difficulty.

00:45:04.300 --> 00:45:21.260
One thing I will also say about the difficulty, not in comparison to Gradius, but something that I think was a good quality of life feature was once the level's over, you get a rest period before you go into the boss.

00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:26.260
Like you can put the controller down for a second, loosen up your joints.

00:45:26.540 --> 00:45:27.800
And then have a seizure.

00:45:27.800 --> 00:45:39.140
I was just going to say that because we've had some seizure inducing things in some of these games, but this was like, I kid you not, when it gets to the point where that slowdown happens, I turn around.

00:45:39.300 --> 00:45:44.400
I look away. I'm not even kidding because I can't handle how quickly these flashing lights come at you.

00:45:44.660 --> 00:45:47.380
I turn around and watch the shadows on my wall flash until the rocks.

00:45:47.380 --> 00:45:50.160
And then you imagine it happening on like a CRT, you know?

00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:54.860
Yeah, right. A big one back in the day and the kids like writing up on the screen.

00:45:54.860 --> 00:45:57.320
And it's very similar, not very similar.

00:45:57.800 --> 00:46:00.420
It's similar to me only because I've been playing it recently.

00:46:00.680 --> 00:46:07.580
But the game Thumper, which is just this like, not theory inducing.

00:46:07.780 --> 00:46:14.420
It puts you in a sort of manic zen state, I guess like a state of flow.

00:46:14.900 --> 00:46:16.360
It's kind of a rhythm game, right?

00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:24.480
It's a rhythm game, but it's also, it tests your endurance in terms of like how hard you can grip a controller.

00:46:24.860 --> 00:46:31.140
But every few segments of a level, they will just give you like a 30 second rest period.

00:46:31.400 --> 00:46:37.120
And I think that with games with this sort of twitch needed in them, it's good to have that just built in.

00:46:37.760 --> 00:46:39.940
But that's, that's all I had to say on that.

00:46:40.100 --> 00:46:41.180
Oh, okay. That was nice.

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:43.160
I like that. And we got a chance to talk about seizures.

00:46:43.500 --> 00:46:44.380
Yeah, I love seizing.

00:46:44.480 --> 00:46:49.320
Seizing. I don't think anybody ever had a seizure playing this game, but I can't say that with certainty.

00:46:49.640 --> 00:46:50.880
People probably can. Yeah.

00:46:51.660 --> 00:46:54.400
So if you guys will humor me, I'm going to go into the whole like.

00:46:54.540 --> 00:46:59.040
Deep lore. Yeah, the sequels and spinoffs and other franchise related things.

00:46:59.200 --> 00:47:00.760
I'm just going to unplug my mic. Great.

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:04.100
Well, I'd love to hear your reactions, if you don't mind.

00:47:05.160 --> 00:47:07.340
Gasp. You know, because I think this stuff is interesting.

00:47:07.580 --> 00:47:10.360
So obviously Stinger, as we mentioned, is part of the Twin Bee line.

00:47:10.640 --> 00:47:12.300
And we'll never get another one.

00:47:12.940 --> 00:47:15.080
We will never get another one in the United States.

00:47:16.360 --> 00:47:18.380
Mostly just because of how Konami is.

00:47:18.380 --> 00:47:20.040
And also because it never happened.

00:47:20.480 --> 00:47:21.460
Twin Bee 3.

00:47:22.360 --> 00:47:26.060
Twin Bee 3 Poco Poco Daimau released.

00:47:26.800 --> 00:47:31.200
Daimau. I don't know why I said that weird, but I was expecting some extra vowels.

00:47:31.940 --> 00:47:34.760
That released on the Famicom in 1989.

00:47:35.580 --> 00:47:39.460
So maybe one day we'll like explore that as a bonus episode or something.

00:47:39.780 --> 00:47:41.940
See how they see the formula got better.

00:47:42.580 --> 00:47:45.180
I didn't check that one out before the show, so that didn't happen.

00:47:45.180 --> 00:47:47.920
But Twin Bee 3 is the last one for the Famicom.

00:47:48.080 --> 00:47:56.240
And then the Twin Bee series continues on the Game Boy, the Super Nintendo, the PlayStation, the Sega Saturn.

00:47:56.460 --> 00:47:58.780
Wow. But all Japan only.

00:47:59.360 --> 00:48:02.240
Never any of these games came to North America again.

00:48:02.560 --> 00:48:03.660
So it makes me wonder.

00:48:03.820 --> 00:48:05.320
Are there sales figures for Stinger?

00:48:05.320 --> 00:48:08.720
There are no sales figures for Stinger that I could get access to.

00:48:09.500 --> 00:48:15.180
But I have to imagine that in a library with only two other shmups, it must have sold okay.

00:48:15.340 --> 00:48:20.220
Yeah. Especially when one of them is its own company's game.

00:48:20.800 --> 00:48:22.140
How bad could it have done?

00:48:22.140 --> 00:48:31.420
But everything ends for Twin Bee with Twin Bee RPG on PlayStation 1.

00:48:32.200 --> 00:48:40.360
A spinoff of the Twin Bee shoot-'em-up series featuring a storyline based on the Twin Bee Paradise audio drama serial.

00:48:40.720 --> 00:48:42.040
It was a radio play.

00:48:42.100 --> 00:48:45.160
Yes. That brings me right into the radio drama.

00:48:45.560 --> 00:48:50.060
Began airing on the radio station NCB on October 10, 1993.

00:48:50.060 --> 00:48:52.200
Again, this is Japanese only, Kurt.

00:48:52.200 --> 00:48:54.660
Yes. Okay. We'll never understand what they said.

00:48:55.060 --> 00:48:58.000
We'll never be able to know the plot.

00:48:58.140 --> 00:49:00.400
But the series lasted three seasons.

00:49:00.760 --> 00:49:01.980
How long is a season?

00:49:02.220 --> 00:49:03.500
Right. That's what I wanted to know, too.

00:49:03.720 --> 00:49:05.000
How much plot could there be?

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:10.780
The third and final season concluded on March 30, 1997.

00:49:11.740 --> 00:49:17.760
So it comprised of a total of 96 episodes, which were later released in CD collections.

00:49:17.760 --> 00:49:21.060
Wow. So if you're a Twin Bee fan, you can get in on that.

00:49:21.260 --> 00:49:23.740
But you're like, Mike, I don't know Japanese.

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:27.620
Clearly nobody is going to reread all 96 episodes in English.

00:49:28.640 --> 00:49:30.620
But I heard there's an anime.

00:49:30.980 --> 00:49:32.680
Oh. Yeah, there's a Twin Bee anime.

00:49:33.020 --> 00:49:33.860
You heard that, right?

00:49:34.020 --> 00:49:35.060
And that makes sense, right?

00:49:35.140 --> 00:49:36.320
It's so cartoony.

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:38.780
That makes a lot more sense than a radio drama.

00:49:38.780 --> 00:49:40.000
Than a radio drama, right?

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:48.800
Because what's the decision making there where you're like, oh, there's probably a lot of crossover between our shoot-em-up game and people who want to listen to radio drama.

00:49:48.800 --> 00:49:50.360
You don't know Japan in the 90s.

00:49:50.480 --> 00:49:59.000
I know. That's got to be something that we just don't have a handle on because I'm struggling to understand the concept of a 90s radio play.

00:49:59.420 --> 00:50:00.540
Was that a thing? I mean.

00:50:00.620 --> 00:50:01.500
The masked.

00:50:02.220 --> 00:50:03.460
What was that?

00:50:03.860 --> 00:50:05.040
No, no. That's from the 30s.

00:50:05.200 --> 00:50:06.120
But, you know, like the.

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:07.520
The masked avenger.

00:50:07.720 --> 00:50:08.600
The lone ranger.

00:50:09.640 --> 00:50:10.840
The masked avenger.

00:50:11.020 --> 00:50:15.880
Is that what you said? The anime was interesting because we got two films.

00:50:16.220 --> 00:50:19.620
Okay. One that ties into the Super Famicom game.

00:50:20.020 --> 00:50:21.080
One that crossover.

00:50:21.500 --> 00:50:23.560
Yep. With Dragon Ball Z.

00:50:23.720 --> 00:50:27.660
No, the other one is it ties in with the OVA series.

00:50:28.980 --> 00:50:31.900
And the OVA series is Twin Bee Paradise.

00:50:32.740 --> 00:50:35.140
It's based on the radio drama of the same name.

00:50:35.240 --> 00:50:36.940
So if you can understand where I'm going with all this.

00:50:36.940 --> 00:50:45.140
Because basically we have a radio drama that had 96 episodes that then got an anime which had only three episodes.

00:50:46.200 --> 00:50:51.020
And then that radio drama which then became an anime also got a film.

00:50:52.260 --> 00:50:54.060
So there's all that going on.

00:50:54.200 --> 00:50:55.960
And then, oh, there's a manga as well.

00:50:56.100 --> 00:51:00.020
So if you want to read the Twin Bee manga, you can also get in on that.

00:51:00.080 --> 00:51:01.640
They pushed hard with this thing.

00:51:01.640 --> 00:51:04.600
Yeah. It's – I watched an episode.

00:51:05.120 --> 00:51:06.420
It was all in Japanese.

00:51:06.680 --> 00:51:08.140
There wasn't even an English sub.

00:51:09.060 --> 00:51:10.620
But I watched an episode.

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:15.080
And I'll tell you it was bizarre and didn't resemble anything I could understand.

00:51:15.400 --> 00:51:17.360
Were they fighting household objects?

00:51:17.460 --> 00:51:18.500
They were barely fighting.

00:51:18.660 --> 00:51:21.840
There was just a lot – like there was a lot of talking.

00:51:22.540 --> 00:51:25.280
There was a lot of like hanging out at a cafe.

00:51:25.740 --> 00:51:26.720
Cafe. Yeah.

00:51:27.160 --> 00:51:39.560
And the ships that they get in – oh, and how could we forget that the OVA, which was based on the radio drama, which was then turned into the Twin Bee RPG on PS1.

00:51:39.740 --> 00:51:42.620
So it's like everything comes full circle with this.

00:51:43.300 --> 00:51:46.840
Guys, are you sad that you're never going to be able to experience half this stuff?

00:51:46.840 --> 00:51:50.700
I could barely – I could barely understand half of that.

00:51:50.840 --> 00:51:53.340
It was just a flood of information.

00:51:53.780 --> 00:51:58.160
Yeah. I feel like just knowing all that stuff exists is entertainment enough for me.

00:51:58.500 --> 00:52:00.000
I live for this stuff.

00:52:00.020 --> 00:52:06.140
It was like when we had a 20-minute talk about Athena and then a 40-minute talk about everything she's been in.

00:52:06.140 --> 00:52:08.540
Yeah. Okay.

00:52:08.540 --> 00:52:11.900
Let's go right into the Essential Games List.

00:52:14.940 --> 00:52:19.060
I have a feeling that this is going to be a tough one for some of you people.

00:52:19.740 --> 00:52:23.180
I have – people know my stance on the Essential Games List now if they visit the website.

00:52:23.960 --> 00:52:31.840
I kind of cleared that up in the Metroid talk where I was kind of like, look, the Essential Games List is going through a change right now.

00:52:32.160 --> 00:52:34.620
Let's make sure we don't screw it up with Stinger.

00:52:34.820 --> 00:52:36.860
So, Joe, take me through it.

00:52:36.860 --> 00:52:40.180
So, you mentioned that the Essential Games List is going through a change.

00:52:40.340 --> 00:52:47.920
And I think that's kind of alluding to the fact that – I talked to Mike earlier and mentioned that I've been rethinking at least the way that I look at the Essential Games List.

00:52:48.500 --> 00:52:55.300
Because we always say it's about what's worth playing today, but we don't necessarily always represent it that way.

00:52:56.520 --> 00:53:06.200
Sometimes that can be really tough, but I do think that when we get to the Best of 87 episode, I'm going to have at least some suggestions for kind of a spring cleaning.

00:53:06.620 --> 00:53:13.980
I don't think it'll actually be spring, but I'll have some suggestions for a cleaning of the Essential Games List.

00:53:14.800 --> 00:53:20.620
So, with that in mind, this game was great, and we obviously are all very high on it, and we compared it to Gradius a lot.

00:53:20.620 --> 00:53:28.340
But I think the major difference between this and Gradius for me, which I talked about a little bit already, was the weird difficulty curve.

00:53:28.760 --> 00:53:37.700
Because as fun as I thought the power-ups were, and interesting as they were, I found that if I had a bunch of power-ups, the game was really easy.

00:53:37.880 --> 00:53:42.220
I mean, specifically some of the boss battles that we talked about, what was it, Sigmund, Squidman.

00:53:42.220 --> 00:53:46.860
And so some of those boss battles were just a little too straightforward and too easy with the power-ups.

00:53:46.980 --> 00:53:55.120
And then on the flip side, when you die and lose the power-ups, the difficulty spike was so crazy that it almost made me want to start the game over just to get the power-ups back.

00:53:55.360 --> 00:54:00.900
So that created a little bit of an unbalance that is ultimately why I kind of lost interest in the game and didn't even finish.

00:54:01.480 --> 00:54:04.080
And that's kind of what sets it apart from Gradius for me.

00:54:04.220 --> 00:54:10.740
And to be totally honest, I obviously really enjoyed this game, but when I stopped to think about it, I can't really see myself ever playing it again.

00:54:10.740 --> 00:54:15.180
I loved playing it once, and I'm very high on it right now, so I definitely think it's a play it.

00:54:15.740 --> 00:54:20.020
Probably as close as a play it can come to being essential without exactly making it.

00:54:20.080 --> 00:54:21.720
So I think it just sort of misses the mark for me.

00:54:22.220 --> 00:54:23.220
All right, Sean?

00:54:24.020 --> 00:54:33.940
Okay, I would like to preface this with a statement that, once again, I do not appreciate being bullied into voting the way that you want, Mike.

00:54:35.100 --> 00:54:36.460
I dislike this.

00:54:37.540 --> 00:54:40.640
Like, you know, we want this, so vote accordingly.

00:54:40.740 --> 00:54:41.680
Like, I get it.

00:54:41.840 --> 00:54:43.280
I get how it's supposed to be.

00:54:43.540 --> 00:54:45.680
And I try my best to get it that way.

00:54:45.720 --> 00:54:47.660
Just so you know, this is actually what I wanted.

00:54:47.720 --> 00:54:48.520
The drama.

00:54:48.840 --> 00:54:51.460
Okay? I can't wait for this to be on every subreddit.

00:54:51.460 --> 00:54:52.500
You're just a manipulator, Mike.

00:54:52.660 --> 00:54:53.720
Every game in the subreddit.

00:54:54.040 --> 00:54:54.860
Because that's the thing.

00:54:54.960 --> 00:55:00.540
It's like, everything I just said was written down, but now it sounds like my opinion was covered.

00:55:00.540 --> 00:55:01.660
This whole thing is scripted.

00:55:01.820 --> 00:55:04.980
And the funny thing, I said a lot of that stuff to you earlier, Mike.

00:55:05.440 --> 00:55:08.660
It's true. Sean saying this whole thing is scripted was part of the script.

00:55:08.660 --> 00:55:10.440
It's actually line 83.

00:55:11.180 --> 00:55:12.320
It's only 83.

00:55:12.520 --> 00:55:13.580
How wide is the paper?

00:55:13.580 --> 00:55:14.540
You don't get many lines, Sean.

00:55:14.820 --> 00:55:15.860
Whatever. All right.

00:55:16.420 --> 00:55:26.020
So, again, if we're going to talk about the Essential Games list as a concept, in all honesty, I play more modern games than I play retro games.

00:55:26.020 --> 00:55:42.780
If the Essential Games list was just for, to me, games that I would just pop on at any moment and not have an asterisk that said it's only retro games, there'd be like five that I would add total, probably.

00:55:42.780 --> 00:55:48.140
Exactly. So, no, I'm giving these games a bit more leeway.

00:55:49.000 --> 00:55:54.840
Because if I'm going to have my NES, I'm going to be playing Stinger.

00:55:55.680 --> 00:56:22.620
And I think that, going back into what I sort of had prepared in my head before we started talking about the Essential Games list in the macro level, I think that Stinger, while it is sort of the less polished, goofy cousin of Gradius, I had a significantly more amount.

00:56:22.620 --> 00:56:27.200
I had a significantly bigger amount of fun with Stinger than I did with Gradius.

00:56:27.400 --> 00:56:31.900
I still really liked Gradius, and I still think it deserves to be an Essential Game.

00:56:32.900 --> 00:56:39.560
And the idea that the Essential Games list at this point would have two shmups on it is weird to me that I voted for.

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:42.480
But I am voting for Stinger to be an Essential Game.

00:56:42.540 --> 00:56:44.140
I had a blast at this game.

00:56:44.260 --> 00:56:46.480
I played it longer than I played a lot of these games.

00:56:47.600 --> 00:56:48.720
So, yeah, that's my vote.

00:56:49.040 --> 00:56:49.900
I love it.

00:56:50.580 --> 00:56:51.740
It's very interesting.

00:56:51.740 --> 00:57:02.480
And I think everything that you said, I think the biggest takeaway for me is that, and this is important, the Essential Games list is different to each person, and it should be.

00:57:02.640 --> 00:57:04.740
That's what makes it such a fun and interesting thing.

00:57:04.740 --> 00:57:09.100
If we all come in and just say, vote how Mike wants it, right, that's weird.

00:57:09.200 --> 00:57:11.340
But we might as well just ask me my opinion.

00:57:11.480 --> 00:57:17.140
So I like that, like, your idea of the Essential Games list is different than Joe's idea, which is different than mine.

00:57:17.140 --> 00:57:23.500
And, like, I'm sure the listeners at home, I've had people tell me, I can't believe Wrecking Crew isn't on the Essential Games list.

00:57:23.560 --> 00:57:25.780
You know, it's like everybody's going to have an opinion.

00:57:25.780 --> 00:57:33.820
And at the end of the day, we're just trying to come up with, like, some kind of, you know, NES canon, right, that works in modern times.

00:57:33.840 --> 00:57:36.500
Not like, oh, these were the best games at that time.

00:57:36.520 --> 00:57:38.320
These are the games that are still fun.

00:57:38.420 --> 00:57:39.960
That's how I view the Essential Games list.

00:57:39.980 --> 00:57:41.560
It's still fun. These are the games that are still fun.

00:57:41.740 --> 00:57:47.680
And I said this in a post on the Metroid on NostalgiaCast.com.

00:57:47.800 --> 00:57:56.860
What I said was an Essential Game is a game that, even though it's limited to the scope and constraints of the system it was designed for,

00:57:57.100 --> 00:58:02.440
it still brings great gameplay, tight controls, and pushes the medium forward.

00:58:02.440 --> 00:58:14.640
And if we're considering the medium here, NES, like, you know, not like even just video games, but just, like, pushes the whole conversation of, like, what this home console experience is with the NES, I'm going to vote yes.

00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:20.100
I'm going to put this thing on the Essential Games list absolutely because I think that it fits all three of those things.

00:58:20.600 --> 00:58:26.560
And I think that, you know, an argument keeps coming back to Metroid because I didn't vote for that on the list.

00:58:26.580 --> 00:58:29.560
And that's because I didn't think it had great gameplay and tight controls.

00:58:29.560 --> 00:58:39.220
I thought it had good gameplay and fun controls, but I don't think it still held up as well in, you know, in modern times as the later Metroid games do.

00:58:39.660 --> 00:58:44.740
This, on the other hand, I think is, like, it's the right amount of, like, fun and cartoonish.

00:58:44.840 --> 00:58:48.000
And, like, the gameplay is consistent throughout.

00:58:48.500 --> 00:58:51.560
And even though the difficulty ramps, it ramps accordingly.

00:58:52.020 --> 00:58:53.680
Stage 1 is easy.

00:58:53.820 --> 00:58:55.800
Stage 2 is just a little tougher.

00:58:55.800 --> 00:58:58.160
It's not like all of a sudden you're just met with this wall.

00:58:58.320 --> 00:58:59.220
There's continues.

00:58:59.560 --> 00:59:00.860
The bosses are fun.

00:59:01.000 --> 00:59:04.720
They're beatable without, like, you know, a wide range of power-ups.

00:59:04.860 --> 00:59:10.920
There's so much that this game is offering that I like it just as much, if not more than Gradius.

00:59:11.040 --> 00:59:12.520
But I'm going to say just as much.

00:59:12.820 --> 00:59:16.280
And I don't care, like, what genres get fulfilled.

00:59:16.500 --> 00:59:24.320
I don't think at the end of, like, this nostalgia we have to look at, like, okay, so 716 games and we never put a puzzle game on it.

00:59:24.380 --> 00:59:25.380
It's like, who cares?

00:59:25.400 --> 00:59:26.420
We're never going to put a puzzle.

00:59:26.420 --> 00:59:28.260
It's game yet. Tetris is going to show up.

00:59:29.300 --> 00:59:33.040
Anyway, I think, like, that's why I'm putting this on the essential games list.

00:59:33.220 --> 00:59:37.000
And 2 out of 3 does put it on our essential games list.

00:59:37.080 --> 00:59:38.240
So it is up there.

00:59:40.100 --> 00:59:42.700
But I think, again, this is a fluid conversation.

00:59:42.840 --> 00:59:47.520
I'm not saying that Stinger will still be on my mind 2, 3 years from now.

00:59:47.640 --> 00:59:51.780
This could be something that because I'm experiencing it for the very first time right now, I'm really hot on it.

00:59:51.860 --> 00:59:57.540
And a game that I would have given a great review back in 1987 and I'm giving it a great review right now.

00:59:57.540 --> 01:00:01.440
Who knows if I'm going to still love it in 2 years after we've played a lot more.

01:00:01.660 --> 01:00:05.300
I think that's the interesting thing about anybody's list of favorite games.

01:00:05.560 --> 01:00:11.520
It's like, when I was a kid, nothing was taking Kingdom Hearts 2 off my number 1 game of all time.

01:00:12.000 --> 01:00:17.700
Now, it's still a great game to me, but I don't think it's, like, the most polished, defined game ever.

01:00:17.700 --> 01:00:18.800
I was a kid. Yeah.

01:00:18.800 --> 01:00:20.200
You know, I like different things.

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:22.060
And you're going to continue to like different things.

01:00:22.260 --> 01:00:25.100
And, you know, Joe, I feel like you want to say something.

01:00:25.140 --> 01:00:35.400
No, I feel like in general with a lot of games, like, especially in the past, like, I feel like I'm a lot of times more likely to give games a pass on things.

01:00:35.640 --> 01:00:37.860
And that's one thing that I've been kind of looking back on.

01:00:37.940 --> 01:00:41.180
Like, some games that are essential, I'm like, am I ever going to play that again?

01:00:41.260 --> 01:00:43.460
Like, do I even care about some of these games?

01:00:43.620 --> 01:00:44.740
So I'm kind of thinking about this.

01:00:44.740 --> 01:00:48.840
Like, I did have a lot of fun playing it, but, like, I mean, I'm probably never going to play this game again.

01:00:49.000 --> 01:00:52.960
Like, unless it's, like, unless I'm sitting there with only, like, NES games.

01:00:53.080 --> 01:00:55.640
But, like, if I have options to play other things, this is probably not on my list.

01:00:55.660 --> 01:01:02.740
I just struggle to think of, and maybe this is who I am as a gamer, I very rarely replay any games.

01:01:03.600 --> 01:01:04.940
Like, there are very few games.

01:01:05.020 --> 01:01:07.640
Like, I love Metroid Prime.

01:01:07.900 --> 01:01:09.540
I love The Witcher 3.

01:01:10.200 --> 01:01:13.620
I love Star Wars Knights of the Old Republic.

01:01:13.620 --> 01:01:19.600
Like, those are three games that are just, like, I don't, I played them each once, and I doubt I'm going back to them.

01:01:19.640 --> 01:01:22.040
Well, I've actually played KOTOR a few times.

01:01:22.320 --> 01:01:25.580
I mean, I can be that way with games, too, but this is a game that I haven't even finished.

01:01:26.000 --> 01:01:27.440
I can go back and finish it.

01:01:27.560 --> 01:01:34.240
